<worksheet >
    <title>MD-3 Determinants and Cramer's Rule</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>To earn an M on this outcome, complete all three problems. 
      Complete problem 3 and either problem 1 or 2 with progress on the other to earn a P.</p>
  </introduction>  
   <page> 
      <exercise workspace="2.5in"><p>Consider the matrix <md alignment="alignat" alignat-columns="3">
            <mrow>A=\begin{bmatrix} 3 &amp; 1 &amp; 2\\1 &amp; 2 &amp; 4\\-1 &amp; -1 &amp; -1 \end{bmatrix}</mrow></md>
            Use the cofactor expansion with row 2 to compute its determinant. Show your work.</p></exercise>
      <exercise workspace="2.5in"><p>Consider the matrix <md alignment="alignat" alignat-columns="3">
            <mrow>B=\begin{bmatrix} 6 &amp; 3 &amp; -1\\2&amp;1 &amp; 4\\ 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 \end{bmatrix}</mrow></md>
            Use the diagonalization method to compute its determinant. Show your work.</p></exercise>
     </page>
     <page>
      <exercise workspace="2.5in"><p>Given the system <md><mrow>2x+3y=8</mrow><mrow>5x-y=3</mrow></md>, 
        use Cramer's Rule to solve for <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Show your work.</p></exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>